---
import { shortcutOrShelter as experiment } from "../data/experiments";

const { results } = experiment;
---

<section class="article-section results-section" aria-labelledby="results-title">
  <div class="section-rail"><span>08</span><p>EMPIRICAL RESULTS</p></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="table-label">FULL RUNS · {experiment.primaryTrials.toLocaleString("en-US")} INDEPENDENT POLICIES</p>
    <h2 id="results-title">The learned boundary depends on the backup.</h2>
    <p class="result-lead">
      In the recoverable maze, the corridor crossed 50% of seeds at p = 0.823 for
      Q-learning, 0.848 for SARSA, and 0.853 for Expected SARSA. In the lethal maze,
      Q-learning crossed only at p = 0.999; neither on-policy method crossed at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each point below is the fraction of 20 independently trained policies whose
      final greedy start action was EAST. The line is not one policy mixing routes;
      it records disagreement across seeds after the same 100,000-interaction budget.
      The stationary ordering matches the continuation objective in the update; it is
      not a general ordering of the algorithms.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="results-figure">
    <img
      src={results.routeSelectionFigure}
      alt="Corridor-selection rates across reliability for Q-learning, SARSA, and Expected SARSA in recoverable and lethal hazard conditions"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Figure 2.</strong> Final route selection by reliability, with 95% Wilson
      intervals across 20 training seeds. Vertical references mark the exact greedy
      and persistent-ε boundaries. An empirical boundary is the 50% crossing of the
      seed-level corridor-selection curve.
      <span class="figure-downloads">
        <a href={results.routeSelectionFigure} download>Figure (SVG)</a>
        <a href={results.boundaryData} download>Boundary data (CSV)</a>
      </span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose result-summary">
    <p>
      In the recoverable maze, the seed-paired median boundary shift relative to
      Q-learning was +0.026 for SARSA (95% interval [0.015, 0.0395]) and +0.0305 for
      Expected SARSA ([0.015, 0.0397]). The lethal on-policy boundaries were
      right-censored, so no numerical shift is imputed there.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="boundary-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Learned route boundaries" tabindex="0">
    <p class="table-label">MODEL TARGETS AND FINITE-BUDGET ESTIMATES</p>
    <table class="boundary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Hazard</th>
          <th scope="col">Method</th>
          <th scope="col">Exact target</th>
          <th scope="col">Learned p50 boundary</th>
          <th scope="col">Reading</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {results.boundaryRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.hazard}</td>
            <td><strong>{row.algorithm}</strong></td>
            <td>{row.modelTarget}</td>
            <td><strong>{row.learnedBoundary}</strong></td>
            <td>{row.reading}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="boundary-key">
      Intervals resample training seeds as the independent unit. “No crossing” means
      the observed corridor-selection rate did not reach 50% on the declared grid;
      it is not extrapolated beyond the data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <aside class="consequence-checks" aria-label="Consequences of learned route choice">
    <article>
      <p class="table-label">RECOVERABLE · p = 0.825 · FROZEN GREEDY</p>
      <h3>Route choice changed exposure.</h3>
      <p>
        Mean return (95% seed interval) was 6.925 [6.866, 6.987] for Q-learning,
        7.040 [6.981, 7.090] for SARSA, and 7.044 [7.003, 7.081] for Expected SARSA.
        Hazard-penalty contacts per 1,000 steps were 50.76 [32.95, 68.25], 15.50
        [5.62, 27.90], and 15.80 [6.99, 26.90], respectively.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="table-label">LETHAL · p = 1 · TWO EVALUATION POLICIES</p>
      <h3>Exploration changed the comparison.</h3>
      <p>
        Frozen greedy policies had zero failures; Q-learning returned 7.52 against
        7.28 for both on-policy methods. With ε = 0.10 still active, Q-learning
        returned 3.787 [3.176, 4.389] with a 0.234 [0.196, 0.274] failure rate,
        versus 7.171 [7.103, 7.208] and 0.002 [0, 0.006] for SARSA, and 7.144
        [7.045, 7.211] and 0.004 [0, 0.010] for Expected SARSA.
      </p>
    </article>
  </aside>

  <div class="result-subhead">
    <p class="table-label">MECHANISM CHECKS</p>
    <h3>One mechanism survived its check. One did not.</h3>
    <p>
      These checks separate two claims. Expected SARSA should remove next-action
      sampling variance at a matched backup. Annealing ε to zero should make the
      trained policies approach the same greedy route. Only the first held here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="results-figure">
    <img
      src={results.mechanismFigure}
      alt="Mechanism checks comparing backup-target variance and route selection after exploration anneals"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Figure 3.</strong> Route choice at the two declared annealing points and
      exact backup-target variance at matched states and Q-values. Annealing did not
      make all three methods recover the common greedy route at this budget.
      <span class="figure-downloads">
        <a href={results.mechanismFigure} download>Figure (SVG)</a>
        <a href={results.analysisManifest} download>Analysis record (JSON)</a>
      </span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="claim-adjudication" aria-label="Prediction outcomes">
    <article>
      <p class="claim-status">Mixed</p>
      <h3>Q-learning and the greedy reference</h3>
      <p>
        Q-learning was the first method to cross toward the corridor in both hazard
        laws, as predicted. Its learned boundaries remained 0.017 and 0.010 above the
        exact greedy references, so the objective ordering was right but exact recovery
        was not.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="claim-status">Supported in ordering</p>
      <h3>On-policy methods and the ε-soft reference</h3>
      <p>
        Recoverable-hazard boundaries landed at 0.848 and 0.853 against an exact
        ε-soft boundary of 0.848. Under lethal hazards, both methods remained below
        50% corridor selection, as the no-crossing reference predicted.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="claim-status">Supported</p>
      <h3>Expected backup and target variance</h3>
      <p>
        At the declared comparison points, variance fell from 0.0154 to 0.0088 in the
        recoverable maze and from 0.0382 to zero in the lethal maze. The reduction is
        exactly the next-action sampling component; it is not a sample-efficiency claim.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="claim-status">Not supported</p>
      <h3>Annealing and the common objective</h3>
      <p>
        Removing exploration did not erase the learned separation after 100,000
        interactions. At recoverable p = 0.825, corridor choice was 1/12 for Q-learning,
        2/12 for SARSA, and 2/12 for Expected SARSA; at lethal p = 1, it was 12/12,
        0/12, and 11/12. The exact common greedy optimum remains; this annealing
        procedure did not recover it reliably.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <aside class="analysis-amendment" aria-labelledby="amendment-title">
    <p id="amendment-title">Analysis amendment · 19 Aug 2026</p>
    <div>
      <p>
        The original executable gate stopped if any final start action was tied or
        off-route. It stopped on one WEST action among {results.unresolvedPolicyTotal.toLocaleString("en-US")} primary policies:
        lethal SARSA at p = 0.99, seed 10. That all-or-nothing gate conflicted with the
        declared three-outcome estimand, which already retained <code>other</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before inspecting the boundary estimates, I amended the gate to preserve and
        report <code>other</code>, and to fail only when unresolved actions exceeded 20%
        within a condition. The observed rate was {results.unresolvedPolicyCount} of {results.unresolvedPolicyTotal.toLocaleString("en-US")}
        overall; the exception remains in the published data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <aside class="validation-record" aria-labelledby="validation-title">
    <p id="validation-title">Validation record</p>
    <div>
      <p>
        Artifact integrity, 100,000-interaction budgets, endpoint calibration,
        late-training stability, and the amended unresolved-action gate all passed.
        A strict nonblocking diagnostic found 3 time-limit truncations among 96,000
        held-out episodes (0.003125%), all from the single off-route policy noted above.
        They remain in the summaries and do not alter the primary start-action estimand.
      </p>
      <p>
        No Quick-mode artifact is included. All reported uncertainty treats training
        seeds—not held-out episodes—as the independent units.
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>
</section>
